A **functional interface** is an interface with exactly one abstract method. The optional `@FunctionalInterface` annotation makes the compiler reject a second abstract method.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface MathOp {
    int apply(int a, int b);
}
```

The long way to implement it inline is an **anonymous class**: `new MathOp() { ... }` with a `public` override of `apply`. A **lambda** says the same thing in one line, because the interface has only one method it could mean:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        {{code}}
        System.out.println("add(6, 3) = " + add.apply(6, 3));
        System.out.println("sub(6, 3) = " + sub.apply(6, 3));
        System.out.println("mul(6, 3) = " + mul.apply(6, 3));
    }
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface MathOp {
    int apply(int a, int b);
}
```

```java
MathOp add = new MathOp() {
    @Override
    public int apply(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
};
MathOp sub = (a, b) -> a - b;   // expression body, returned for you
MathOp mul = (a, b) -> {        // block body needs its own return
    int product = a * b;
    return product;
};
```

Parameter types come from the interface. One parameter needs no parentheses (`x -> x * 2`), and no parameters is `() -> 42`.

Gotcha: a lambda can read a local variable of the method around it, but that variable must be **effectively final**, meaning it is never reassigned, before or after the lambda. Add `offset++` anywhere and `(a, b) -> a + b + offset` stops compiling.

example.code:
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MathOp add = new MathOp() {
            @Override
            public int apply(int a, int b) {
                return a + b;
            }
        };
        MathOp subtract = (a, b) -> a - b;
        MathOp multiply = (a, b) -> {
            int product = a * b;
            return product;
        };
        int offset = 100;
        MathOp addOffset = (a, b) -> a + b + offset;

        Map<String, MathOp> ops = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        ops.put("add", add);
        ops.put("subtract", subtract);
        ops.put("multiply", multiply);
        ops.put("addOffset", addOffset);

        for (Map.Entry<String, MathOp> entry : ops.entrySet()) {
            int result = entry.getValue().apply(6, 3);
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + "(6, 3) = " + result);
        }
    }
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface MathOp {
    int apply(int a, int b);
}

