steps.body:
Lists have methods that take a lambda and run it on every element. `removeIf` and `replaceAll` change the list itself, so call them on an `ArrayList`: a `List.of` list throws `UnsupportedOperationException`.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(4, -2, 7, 0, -5, 3));
        {{code}}
    }
}
```

```java
nums.removeIf(n -> n < 0);                  // delete every match
nums.replaceAll(n -> n * 3);                // swap each value for a new one
nums.forEach(n -> System.out.println(n));   // do something with each
```

These lambdas implement ready-made interfaces from `java.util.function`, so you rarely define your own:

| Interface | Method | Takes and returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Predicate<T>` | `test(t)` | a T, returns boolean |
| `Function<T, R>` | `apply(t)` | a T, returns an R |
| `Consumer<T>` | `accept(t)` | a T, returns nothing |

`removeIf` takes a `Predicate` and `forEach` takes a `Consumer`. Store one in a variable and call its method:

```java
import java.util.function.Predicate;

Predicate<Integer> isEven = n -> n % 2 == 0;
isEven.test(4);   // true
```

`Supplier<T>` (`get()`, no input) and `BiFunction<T, U, R>` (`apply(a, b)`, two inputs) follow the same pattern.

Gotcha: `nums.forEach(n -> total += n)` does not compile. A lambda may use a local variable only if it is effectively final, never reassigned, and this one reassigns `total`. Sum with an enhanced for loop instead.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Consumer;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.function.Predicate;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(4, -2, 7, 0, -5, 3));
        nums.removeIf(n -> n < 0);
        nums.replaceAll(n -> n * 3);
        nums.forEach(n -> System.out.println("kept " + n));

        Predicate<Integer> isEven = n -> n % 2 == 0;
        Function<Integer, String> label = n -> "#" + n;
        Consumer<String> show = s -> System.out.println("> " + s);
        for (int n : nums) {
            show.accept(label.apply(n) + " even: " + isEven.test(n));
        }

        int total = 0;
        for (int n : nums) {
            total += n;
        }
        System.out.println("total: " + total);
    }
}

