`list.sort` takes a comparator: a lambda `(a, b) -> ...` that returns a negative number when `a` goes first, zero for a tie, and a positive number when `b` goes first. Return `Integer.compare(a, b)`, never `a - b`, which overflows when the values are far apart.

`java.util.Comparator` builds comparators from a key. A **method reference** like `String::length` is short for `s -> s.length()`, and `Book::getTitle` for `b -> b.getTitle()`.

```java harness
class Book {
    private final String title;
    private final int year;

    Book(String title, int year) {
        this.title = title;
        this.year = year;
    }

    public String getTitle() {
        return title;
    }

    public int getYear() {
        return year;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return title + " (" + year + ")";
    }
}

List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(5, 1, 4, 2));
List<String> words = new ArrayList<>(List.of("pear", "fig", "banana", "kiwi"));
List<Book> books = new ArrayList<>(List.of(new Book("Ubik", 1969), new Book("Dune", 1965), new Book("Contact", 1985)));
{{code}}
System.out.println(nums);
System.out.println(words);
System.out.println(books);
```

```java
import java.util.Comparator;

nums.sort((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a, b));          // small first
words.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)); // short first
books.sort(Comparator.comparing(Book::getTitle));    // A to Z
nums.sort(Comparator.reverseOrder());                // big first
```

- `thenComparing(key)` breaks ties with a second key.
- `reversed()` reverses the **whole chain** built so far.
- `Comparator.comparing(key, Comparator.reverseOrder())` reverses only that key.
- `Comparator.naturalOrder()` sorts values by their own order, `reverseOrder()` the opposite way.
- `Arrays.sort(rows, (a, b) -> ...)` sorts the rows of an `int[][]`.

Always break ties so the printed order is fixed. Gotcha: `Comparator.comparing(s -> s.length()).reversed()` does not compile. Chaining a method onto it stops Java from inferring the lambda's parameter type, so `s` is treated as an `Object`. Use a method reference or one full `(a, b) ->` lambda.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> words = new ArrayList<>(List.of("pear", "fig", "banana", "kiwi", "apple", "plum"));
        words.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length).reversed()
                .thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()));
        System.out.println(words);

        List<Book> books = new ArrayList<>();
        books.add(new Book("Dune", 1965));
        books.add(new Book("Ender's Game", 1985));
        books.add(new Book("Neuromancer", 1984));
        books.add(new Book("Contact", 1985));
        books.add(new Book("Ubik", 1969));
        books.sort(Comparator.comparing(Book::getYear, Comparator.reverseOrder())
                .thenComparing(Book::getTitle));
        System.out.println(books);

        int[][] intervals = {{5, 8}, {1, 4}, {5, 6}, {2, 3}};
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> {
            if (a[0] != b[0]) {
                return Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]);
            }
            return Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]);
        });
        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(intervals));
    }
}

class Book {
    private final String title;
    private final int year;

    Book(String title, int year) {
        this.title = title;
        this.year = year;
    }

    public String getTitle() {
        return title;
    }

    public int getYear() {
        return year;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return title + " (" + year + ")";
    }
}

