Maps have lambda helpers that replace the `getOrDefault` and `putIfAbsent` patterns:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Map<String, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>(Map.of("sea", 1, "tea", 1));
        Map<Integer, List<String>> groups = new TreeMap<>();
        String word = "tea";
        int len = word.length();
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(groups);
    }
}
```

```java
counts.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum);
groups.computeIfAbsent(len, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(word);
counts.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println(k + " " + v));
```

- `merge(key, 1, Integer::sum)` stores 1 for a new key. For a key already there it combines the old value and 1 with `Integer.sum`, the static method that `Integer::sum` refers to.
- `computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>())` builds a list only the first time it sees a key, then returns the list stored there, so you can chain `.add(v)`.
- `forEach((k, v) -> ...)` visits every pair. On a `TreeMap` that is key order, but a `HashMap` promises no order.

To rank entries, copy them into a list and sort it. Java cannot work out the entry types of `comparingByValue(...)` when you chain `thenComparing` straight onto it, so store it in a typed variable first:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Map<String, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>(Map.of("tea", 2, "sea", 1, "ten", 2, "net", 3));
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(entries);
    }
}
```

```java
List<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> entries = new ArrayList<>(counts.entrySet());
Comparator<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> byCount =
        Map.Entry.comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder());
entries.sort(byCount.thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()));
```

That sorts by count, highest first, and breaks ties by key A to Z.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] words = {"tea", "sea", "ten", "net", "seen", "nest", "tease"};

        Map<Character, Integer> letters = new TreeMap<>();
        Map<Integer, List<String>> byLength = new TreeMap<>();
        for (String w : words) {
            for (char c : w.toCharArray()) {
                letters.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
            }
            byLength.computeIfAbsent(w.length(), k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(w);
        }

        letters.forEach((c, n) -> System.out.println(c + ": " + n));
        byLength.forEach((len, list) -> System.out.println(len + " -> " + list));

        List<Map.Entry<Character, Integer>> entries = new ArrayList<>(letters.entrySet());
        Comparator<Map.Entry<Character, Integer>> byCount =
                Map.Entry.comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder());
        entries.sort(byCount.thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()));
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            Map.Entry<Character, Integer> e = entries.get(i);
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + e.getKey() + " " + e.getValue());
        }
    }
}

